{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>In the seventh month.</b> This is Sivan, which is seventh to Kislev, in which the rains ceased. On the tenth, on the first of the month, the mountaintops appeared — this is Av, which is tenth to Marcheshvan, in which the rains began; thus Rashi interpreted. And if you ask: but the year of the flood was a leap year — how do we know? From creation until the flood was 1,656 years; go and calculate the years of the cycles and you will find that the year 1,656 was third in the cycle; if so, Sivan is eighth to Kislev and Av is twelfth to Marcheshvan. Therefore some say the flood began after 1,656. Another interpretation: from where are 1,656 counted? From the year of Tohu, and then the year of the flood was a regular year. Another interpretation: the year of the flood was not counted among the other years — these are the words of Rabbi Yehudah. Rabbi Nechemiah said to him: even though it is not counted in the tally, it is counted in the seasons and calculations." "<b>And the ark rested in the seventh month on the seventeenth day.</b> Rashi interpreted: from here you learn how deeply the ark was submerged in the water, etc. And if you ask: but we know that on the first of Av the mountaintops appeared, and on the first of Tishrei the waters returned to their original order, as it is written, 'בראשון באחד לחדש חרבו המים מעל הארץ' — so in these sixty days the waters diminished by the entire height of the mountains. And it is obvious that there are mountains higher than fifteen cubits, so they diminished more than a cubit per four days. And one cannot answer: since they began to diminish, they diminished a lot — if so, it would not prove the ark's submergence in the water of eleven cubits. Rather, one may answer that the mountains take up much of the open space of the world; therefore they diminished all the heights of the mountains in sixty days, but above the mountains the open space of the world is uniform, and from there and upward they diminished a cubit for every four days, as Rashi interpreted. And a proof for this: we do not find the language of diminishment; it is written: 'משנראו ראשי ההרים' (from when the mountaintops appeared), teaching you that it is not considered a simple diminishment. <b>And Rashi further interpreted:</b> And if you say the seventh is counted from the descent — this is not Sivan; that is, it is not 'seventh' but Iyyar. And if so, how do you interpret 'and the ark rested in Iyyar, the seventh month from the descent, on the seventeenth day of the month'? After all, the surge of the waters had not yet finished, as we said: the forty days of rain and the one hundred fifty of the surge were not complete until the first of Sivan. And this is the schedule of all the days of the year of the flood according to the method of Rashi. In the seventh month — this is Marcheshvan, as we hold in tractate Rosh HaShanah and in Seder Olam: the Sages say, according to the words of Rabbi Eliezer for the flood, and according to the words of Rabbi Yehoshua for the seasons: on the seventeenth day of the month, all the fountains of the great deep burst open, etc.; 'ויהי הגשם על הארץ ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה' — from dawn of the seventeenth of Marcheshvan until dawn of the twenty-eighth of Kislev; 'ויהי המבול ארבעים יום' etc.; 'ויסכרו מעינת תהום' etc.; 'ויגברו המים'; 'ותלך התבה על פני המים'; 'חמש עשרה אמה מלמעלה גברו המים ויכסו ההרים'; 'ויגברו המים על הארץ חמשים ומאת יום' — from dawn of the twenty-eighth of Kislev until nightfall of the first of Sivan. Immediately 'ויזכור אלקים את נח' and the waters began to diminish from the end of one hundred fifty days — that is, they began to diminish at the end of those one hundred fifty days that were completed on the first of Sivan. 'ותנח התבה בחדש השביעי' — in Sivan, which is seventh to the cessation of the rains, 'בשבעה עשר יום לחדש'. <b>והמים היו הלוך וחסור</b> — from the first of Sivan until Av, which is the tenth month from the descent of the rains. 'בעשירי באחד לחדש נראו ראשי ההרים' — that is, the fifteen cubits by which the waters had risen above the mountains diminished from the first of Sivan until the first of Av, at a cubit per four days. <b>ויהי מקץ ארבעים יום</b> — from when the mountaintops appeared — concluded on the tenth of Elul. On that day 'ויפתח נח את חלון התבה אשר עשה', 'וישלח את הערב' etc. 'ויחל שבעת ימים' as Rashi interpreted; 'וישלח את היונה ולא מצאה היונה מנוח לכף רגלה ותשב אליו' etc. 'ויחל עוד שבעת ימים אחרים ויסף שלח את היונה מאתו'. 'ותבא אליו היונה לעת ערב והנה עלה זית טרף בפיה' — which it took from the olive trees on the mountaintops. And some say: from the Mount of Anointing she brought it, for the Land of Israel was not struck by the flood, as it is written: 'לא גושמה ביום זעם'. 'וייחל עוד שבעת ימים אחרים וישלח את היונה ולא יספה שוב אליו עוד'. Immediately 'ויהי באחת ושש מאות שנה בראשון' — on the first of Tishrei, which is 'ראשון', <b>'באחד לחדש חרבו המים'</b> etc., and in Marcheshvan, 'בחדש השני בשבעה ועשרים יום לחדש יבשה הארץ'. Some wonder about Rashi's interpretation: what compelled him to interpret 'מקץ ארבעים יום' — that is, from when the mountaintops appeared? If so, why did the dove not find rest for the sole of her foot? After all, there are forty-seven days from when the mountaintops appeared until the dove was sent, and she could have rested on many mountains. And some reject this, seeking and saying that the face of the valley was not exposed; this is not 'rest'. And by reason one may answer that Rashi's intent was to say that the entire passage was stated in order, and the fact that the dove did not return to him anymore when she was sent the last time — that was on the first of Tishrei, when the waters dried up. Some begin counting the one hundred fifty days from the seventeenth of Marcheshvan, and include in them the forty days of rainfall, and count 'the seventh month from the descent' just as we count 'the tenth from the descent.' And this is what it says: 'ויגברו המים על הארץ חמשים ומאת יום' — for there are [one hundred fifty days] from the sixteenth of Marcheshvan until the twentieth of Nisan; 'ויחסרו המים מקצה חמשים ומאת יום' — which conclude on the twentieth of Nisan, and on that day they began to diminish; 'ותנח התבה' in Iyyar, the seventh month from the descent; 'בעשירי באחד לחדש נראו ראשי ההרים' — and the fifteen cubits above the mountaintops diminished in ninety-five days — for there are [ninety-five days] from the twentieth of Nisan until the first of Av — a cubit per six and a half days. And some say the end of this passage follows the method of Seder Olam: <b>ויהי מקץ ארבעים יום</b> — from when the waters began to diminish, that is, from the first of Sivan until the tenth of Tammuz. Immediately <b>ויפתח נח את חלון התבה</b> etc., 'וישלח את הערב' etc. 'וייחל עוד שבעת ימים וישלח את היונה ולא יספה שוב' — she went and sat on the mountains. That day was the first of Av, as it is written: 'בעשירי באחד לחדש נראו ראשי ההרים' — 'זה אב העשירי למרחשון שבו ירדו הגשמים', since part of the month is like the whole. But one cannot say it refers to Elul, which is tenth to Kislev in which the rains ceased — for if so, at the third sending why did the dove not return to him anymore? Where did she go? After all, you want to say the mountaintops did not appear until the first of Elul. And do not wonder at this verse, <b>והמים היו הלוך וחסור</b> etc. — to say, how is it fixed before the sending of the dove? Rather, since it began to tell of the diminishment of the waters — that they diminished from the first of Sivan until the ark rested — it goes and completes the diminishment until the mountaintops appeared. And from the first of Av until the first of Tishrei the waters dried up, as it is written: 'ויהי באחת ושש מאות שנה בראשון באחד לחדש חרבו המים', and 'בחדש השני בשבעה ועשרים יום לחדש יבשה הארץ'."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {1 "This is a very long, complex chronological comment with many internal cross-references and abbreviated calculations; I have preserved Rashi's citation format ('פרש״י') as written and kept all internal Scripture quotations in Hebrew, resolving abbreviations mechanically (א״ת → and if you ask, י״מ → some say, ד״א → another interpretation, ה״ק → this is what it says, וגו' → etc.). Where the Hebrew uses צ״ה = 95 and similar numerical abbreviations I rendered the number in words."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:03:55.741731677Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}